   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Narrative report</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reporting is an important management tool which is used to inform relevant government officials and other stakeholders about the results being achieved by individual projects and the programme as a whole. 
For project partners, it is compulsory to use the prescribed Orange Knowledge annual report format in order to report on project achievements. The annual report also provides valuable data on the financial situation of the project. Based on an analysis of the project performance in relation to the project output(s) and expected outcome(s), a revision of the project work plan may be suggested by the project partners. The consequences of a possible revision on the project work plan and budget must be made clear in the annual report.
Nuffic monitors outputs and outcomes and not activities. Therefore, the main focus of the annual report must be on the achievement of the project&#x2019;s output(s) and planned outcome(s). The information provided in the annual reports of the projects is used by Nuffic to monitor the progress of the project and to inform the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and other stakeholders about the output(s) and outcome(s) being achieved by individual projects and the Orange Knowledge programme as a whole. 
Please note that if project progress is in accordance with the original planning, reporting requirements are minimal. Only in case of deviations from the planning, more elaboration is demanded.
In the Grant Obligations and Conditions, the obligatory dates for submission of the annual/additional reports are mentioned. The final report must reflect on the entire project period.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2. Project progress and achievement</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In this section (maximum 1000 words), please

**For TMT+:**

Comment to what extent the outputs have been achieved. Comment on the activities that have taken place and the most important milestones.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2019-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2019-12-31"></period-end>
     <actual value="Has described in the proposal we had the intention to organise 2 trainings and we are glad that we have also completed the 2 trainings has it was proposed. We have provided the following training subjects:

**Effective project management (face to face)**

- Project design and execution 

- Monitoring and evaluation

- Key-stakeholders management 

- Decision supporting tools implementation 

&#x200b;

**Training on Efficient Irrigation Water Management (online and face to face)**

- Introduction to the state of the art on-farm irrigation 

- Irrigation water resources modelling 

- Evapotranspiration and crop water requirements 

- Coping with climate change in agriculture 

As aforementioned the biggest bottleneck which caused deviation from our original proposal plan was the fact that COVID-19 pandemic occurred in 2020. Due to the lock-down both in the Netherlands and Afghanistan caused by the COVID-19 pandemic, we were not able to organise the last training on Efficient Irrigation Water Management under the TMT project as it was planned and mentioned in the TMT joint proposal. On top of that, due to travel restrictions and negative travel advise by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, our Dutch trainer were not able to travel to India (where part of the training had to take place) to provide the training. The training-date was postponed several times and finally we made the decision to transfer the course to an online mode and host the course in Kabul, Afghanistan In this regard we asked Nuffic for a Budget Neutral Extension because we needed more time. We provided blended learning training consisting of online and offline sessions. Our Dutch trainers provided their part of the training online and our local trainer provided his part of the training face-to-face taking COVID-19 restrictions in to account. Finally we managed to deliver the desired outcomes and reaching the training objectives has it was described in the proposal plan. 

The trainees gained knowledge on the latest developments of using Earth Observation and dedicated software programmes to monitor, assess and improve Water Management in Irrigated Areas. Dedicated software to obtain climate (meteorological) data, assess irrigated water demand, water productivity (dry yield/m3) and land cover monitoring was introduced. With the gained knowledge the participants can develop and integrate their own irrigation management knowledge and skills with new geospatial tools to acquire, monitor and assess relevant water resources data and information."></actual>
    </period>
